     1	# Cracking the recursion of fractional-parentage coefficients for spinor bosons
     2	
     3	**Book:** 母配分系数递推关系的破解 ("Solve the recurrence relation of the fractional parentage
     4	coefficients"), Bao Chengguang (鲍诚光, Sun Yat-sen University), *10000 Selected Problems in
     5	Sciences: Physics*, Science Press 2009, pp. 348–349 (PDF pp. 381–382). Excerpt:
     6	[source/problem.zh.txt](source/problem.zh.txt).
     7	
     8	**Status as of 2026-10-14 (America/Los_Angeles): PARTIAL.** FRONTIER state:
     9	**PROOF-OBLIGATION** (low-spin conjecture), **NEEDS-NEW-REPRESENTATION** (multiplicity sector),
    10	**NOVELTY-UNCLEAR** (new formulas were not found in the literature checked).
    11	
    12	## The problem in one paragraph
    13	
    14	Coefficients of fractional parentage (CFPs) expand a symmetric N-particle state into one
    15	particle (or a pair) times states of the remaining N−1 (N−2) particles. With them, one- and
    16	two-body matrix elements become simple sums. The book points out that there is no general
    17	analytic formula. CFPs are generated by a recursion in N that becomes unmanageable for the
    18	mesoscopic range N ≈ 10–1000. Only spin-1 bosons have a closed form (Bao & Li 2005; the j = 1
    19	result is older, Marić & Popović-Božić 1980). Other cases are called "very hard". The goal is to
    20	crack the recursion beyond spin 1.
    21	
    22	## Precise formulations attacked (and what they do not settle)
    23	
    24	For N spin-f bosons in total-spin eigenstates labelled by seniority v (the O(2f+1) irrep), a
    25	missing label α, and total spin L:
    26	
    27	1. **The N-recursion, every f.** Closed-form N-dependence of all one-body CFPs (Theorem A).
    28	2. **Infinite families as functions of v.** These are
    29	   (a) near-stretched states L = fv − 2, fv − 3 for every f (Theorem B), and the first
    30	       multiplicity family L = fv − 4 for every f ≥ 3 (Theorem B');
    31	   (b) spin 2, L = 2v − k for k ≤ 8 (Claim C);
    32	   (c) spin 2, low total spin L ≤ 5 at any seniority (Theorem E1, Conjecture E2).
    33	3. **The multiplicity question.** Can the missing label be chosen so that CFPs keep the Bao–Li
    34	   product form? For spin 2: no rational basis does it in the first five multiplicity-2 families
    35	   (Theorem D). For every spin f ≥ 3: yes, the pair-first basis does it in the first
    36	   multiplicity family (Theorem B').
    37	
    38	**Not settled:** a uniform closed form for every (v, α, L) of spin 2; general f beyond the
    39	near-stretched families; spin-3/2 fermions (not attacked); a proof of Conjecture E2. Two-body CFPs
    40	follow from the one-body ones by the standard chain formula, which is verified exactly
    41	(RESULTS Claim G).
    42	
    43	## Verdict, by component
    44	
    45	| Component | Verdict | Evidence |
    46	|---|---|---|
    47	| N-dependence for all f (Theorem A; Bao–Li as f = 1) | ALREADY-SOLVED in substance (standard quasi-spin mechanism); explicit general-f formula and sign rule proved here | Certification + exact checks |
    48	| Near-stretched states, any f ≥ 2 (Theorem B) | SOLVED for k = 2, 3 | Certification (computer-assisted) |
    49	| First multiplicity family, any f ≥ 3 (Theorem B') | SOLVED (Bao–Li-type closed forms in the pair-first basis) | Certification (computer-assisted) + 150 exact checks |
    50	| Spin 2, top families k ≤ 8 (Claim C) | SOLVED family by family (k ≤ 6 reproduces Pan et al. 2014) | Certification + Reproduction |
    51	| Bao–Li form in multiplicity families (Theorem D) | SOLVED with a negative answer (no-go theorem for Q(v)-rational bases, k = 6, 8, 9, 10, 11); scope in RESULTS §D | Certification (exact algebra + bounded search) |
    52	| Spin 2, low spin L ≤ 5, all v | PARTIAL: one family proved (E1); 26 formulas conjectured | Certification (E1); Empirical evidence (E2, 800 exact checks) |
    53	| Full problem (all states, all spins, fermions) | OPEN-BLOCKED | exact blocker in RESULTS §G |
    54	
    55	## Key formulas
    56	
    57	* Theorem A (D = 2f+1): c(N v → N−1, v−1) = √[v(N+v+D−2)/(N(2v+D−2))]·r, and
    58	  c(N v → N−1, v+1) = (−1)^{L+L'} √[(N−v)(v+D−2)/(N(2v+D−2))]·√[dim_v(2L'+1)/(dim_{v+1}(2L+1))]·r',
    59	  where r and r' are N-independent O(D) ⊃ SO(3) isoscalar factors.
    60	* Theorem B, e.g. r²(fv−2 → f(v−1)) = 2(fv−1)/(v(2fv−2f−1)). Full table in RESULTS §B.
    61	* Theorem E1: total weight of a scalar parent = (2v+D−2)(v+D−3)/(Dv(2v+D−4)).
    62	* Theorem D, k = 6: every Q(v)-rational basis carries a non-linear irreducible factor. The
    63	  trio-first basis attains the minimum, one quadratic 18v²+91v−190.
    64	* Theorem B': for f ≥ 3 and L = fv − 4, every CFP in the pair-first basis is a product of factors
    65	  linear in v. The only non-obvious factor is g = f(2f−1)v + 3(4f²−10f+5).
    66	
