    91	## B. Theorem B — near-stretched states of any spin f ≥ 2
    92	
    93	Write a highest-weight state of degree v and S_z = fv − k in "deficit" coordinates
    94	z_f^{v−|d|} Π_j z_{f−j}^{d_j}, with Σ_j j d_j = k. Here z_m creates the unnormalized orbital u^{f+m}v^{f−m},
    95	so its Fock weight is w_{f−j} = 1/binom(2f, j). In these coordinates
    96	S₊ z_{f−j} = j z_{f−j+1} does not depend on v or f. For k < 2f the pair operator P lowers the
    97	deficit by 2f, so every such state is harmonic with seniority v. The highest-weight space has
    98	dimension p(k) − p(k−1) (partition numbers): 1 for k = 2 and k = 3. The states are
    99	ψ₂ = z_f^{v−1}z_{f−2} − z_f^{v−2}z_{f−1}² and
   100	ψ₃ = z_f^{v−1}z_{f−3} − 3z_f^{v−2}z_{f−1}z_{f−2} + 2z_f^{v−3}z_{f−1}³.
   101	
   102	**Theorem B.** For every integer spin f ≥ 2 and every v at which both states exist, with parent
   103	spin L = fv − k and daughter spin L' = f(v−1) − k':
   104	
   105	| k → k' | r² | sign |
   106	|---|---|---|
   107	| 2 → 0 | 2(fv−1) / (v(2fv−2f−1)) = 2(L+1)/(v(2L'−1)) | + |
   108	| 2 → 2 | (v−2)(2fv−1) / (v(2fv−2f−1)) = (v−2)(2L+3)/(v(2L'+3)) | + |
   109	| 3 → 0 | f(v−2)(2fv−3) / (v(fv−f−1)(2fv−2f−1)) = f(v−2)(2L+3)/(v(L'−1)(2L'−1)) | − |
   110	| 3 → 2 | 6(f−1)(fv−1) / (v(fv−f−2)(2fv−2f−1)) = 6(f−1)(L+2)/(vL'(2L'+3)) | + |
   111	| 3 → 3 | (v−3)(fv−2)(fv−1) / (v(fv−f−2)(fv−f−1)) = (v−3)(L+1)(L+2)/(v(L'+1)(L'+2)) | + |
   112	
   113	Signs use the phase "coefficient of the largest single deficit positive". With Theorem A, this
   114	gives every one-body CFP of these states for all N and all f, in Bao–Li form.
   115	
   116	*Proof (computer-assisted).* The states ψ₂ and ψ₃ follow from S₊ψ = 0 (shown above). Every CFP is
   117	a finite sum over at most three monomials with Fock weights (v−|d|)! Π d_j! w_{f−j}^{d_j}. The CG
   118	coefficient of the stretched state is ⟨f μ; L' L'|L L⟩² = Π_{i<j}(2L'−i)/(2L+2+i) with
   119	j = f − μ = k − k'. All ratios of factorials have fixed integer offsets, so each r² is an exact
   120	rational function of (v, f). `top_family_symbolic.py general 3` performs this algebra.
   121	
   122	*Hand check of 2 → 0.* X = ⟨ψ₂, z_{f−2}z_f^{v−1}⟩ = (v−1)! w_{f−2}, ‖ψ₂‖² = (v−2)!(2fv−1)/(2f²(2f−1)),
   123	‖z_f^{v−1}‖² = (v−1)!, w_{f−2} = 1/(f(2f−1)) and CG² = (2fv−2f)(2fv−2f−1)/((2fv−2)(2fv−1)).
   124	Then r² = X²/(w_{f−2}‖ψ₂‖²‖φ‖² v CG²) = 2(fv−1)/(v(2fv−2f−1)), as stated.
   125	
   126	*Verification (ran):* `$PY top_family_symbolic.py general 3` printed the table above.
   127	`$PY check_general_f.py` printed `Theorem B formulas agree exactly (with signs) with the
   128	polynomial engine: 200 checks, f = 2..6, v <= 10`. That engine is a separate implementation: full
   129	monomial spaces with a null-space computation.
   130	*Scope:* k = 2, 3. The case k = 4 is the first multiplicity family for f ≥ 3; it is treated in
   131	Theorem B' below.
   132	
   133	### Theorem B' — the first multiplicity family of every spin f ≥ 3 has Bao–Li-type closed forms
   134	
   135	For f ≥ 3 the states with L = fv − 4 form a two-dimensional space, and both have seniority v
   136	(P lowers the deficit by 2f > 4). Let Q4 and H2 be the degree-2 highest-weight pairs of spin
   137	2f − 4 and 2f − 2. The **pair-first basis** is the Gram–Schmidt basis of
   138	φ_A = z_f^{v−2}Q4, followed by φ_B = z_f^{v−4}H2². Set g(v) = f(2f−1)v + 3(4f²−10f+5) and
   139	g⁻ = g(v−1). Daughters k' = 4 use the same rule at v − 1. For every integer f ≥ 3 and v ≥ 5:
   140	
   141	| parent → daughter | r² | sign |
   142	|---|---|---|
   143	| 1 → k'=0 | (2f−1)(fv−3)(fv−2)(2fv−5)(2fv−3) / (v(fv−f−1)(2fv−2f−3)(2fv−2f−1) g) | + |
   144	| 1 → k'=2 | 12f(f−1)(2f−3)(v−2)(fv−3)(2fv−5) / (v(fv−f−2)(2fv−2f−5)(2fv−2f−1) g) | + |
   145	| 1 → k'=3 | 2f²(2f−3)(2f−1)(v−3)(v−2)(fv−3) / (v(fv−f−3)(fv−f−2)(fv−f−1) g) | + |
   146	| 1 → k'=4, 1 | (v−2) g / (v g⁻) | + |
   147	| 1 → k'=4, 2 | 12f⁴(f−1)(2f−3)(2f−1)(v−4)(v−3)(v−2) / (v(fv−f−3)(2fv−2f−5)(2fv−2f−3) g⁻ g) | − |
   148	| 2 → k'=0 | 12f²(f−1)(2f−3)(v−3)(v−2)(fv−2) / (v(fv−f−1)(2fv−2f−3)(2fv−2f−1) g) | − |
   149	| 2 → k'=2 | 4f(2f−1)(v−3)(2fv−3)(fv+2f−5)² / (v(fv−f−2)(2fv−2f−5)(2fv−2f−1) g) | + |
   150	| 2 → k'=3 | 24(f−1)³(2fv−5)(2fv−3) / (v(fv−f−3)(fv−f−2)(fv−f−1) g) | − |
   151	| 2 → k'=4, 1 | 0 | |
   152	| 2 → k'=4, 2 | (v−4)(fv−3)(2fv−5)(2fv−3) g⁻ / (v(fv−f−3)(2fv−2f−5)(2fv−2f−3) g) | + |
   153	
   154	Every factor is linear in v, so for each fixed f all ten CFPs are of Bao–Li type, and the two rows
   155	sum to 1 exactly. Together with Theorem A this gives closed forms for all N. This **contrasts with
   156	spin 2** (Theorem D): there the first multiplicity family (k = 6) has no Bao–Li-type rational basis.
   157	The difference comes from the SO(3)-scalar trio j, which exists for f = 2.
   158	*Proof (computer-assisted):* the same exact symbolic algebra in (v, f) as for Theorem B
   159	(`top_family_symbolic.run_general_k4`; completeness checked symbolically). All overlaps X have
   160	factors that are manifestly positive for v ≥ 5, f ≥ 3, so the signs hold on the whole range.
   161	*Ran:* `$PY check_general_f.py` printed `Theorem B' (k = 4, pair-first basis) agrees exactly (with
   162	signs; completeness = 1): 150 CFPs, f = 3, 4, 5, v = 5..9`, against the independent polynomial
   163	engine. For f = 3, 4, 5 the script `general_f_k4.py` confirms the square-class picture:
   164	q(φ_A) is linear in v, Δ is a product of linear factors, and the residue search finds solutions.
   165	*Novelty:* NOVELTY-UNCLEAR. We found no general-f CFP closed forms in the literature checked.
